<TopicPage slug="subjects/physics/advanced/fluid-dynamics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Fluid dynamics tracks stuff that flows, and its two master laws are beautifully simple.
    <strong>Conservation of mass (continuity):</strong> what goes in must come out — squeeze the
    pipe and the fluid must speed up. <strong>Conservation of energy (Bernoulli):</strong> faster
    flow costs pressure. Between them they explain flight, plumbing, blood flow and why the
    shower curtain attacks you.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="continuity at work — halve the pipe radius, quadruple the speed (area falls as r²)" widget="plotter" config={contCfg} />

  <p>
    With 10 litres per second flowing, a 20 cm pipe idles at 0.08 m/s while an 8 cm neck screams
    at 5 m/s. Squeeze harder still and the speed climbs as 1/r² — the maths behind river gorges,
    nozzle jets and garden-hose physics.
  </p>

  <h2>The highlights</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Bernoulli's principle:</strong> where flow is faster, pressure is lower. Plane wings, curveballs and carburettors use it — though lift is really circulation plus deflection, not just "fast air sucks".</li>
    <li><strong>Viscosity and Reynolds number:</strong> Re = ρvL/μ decides everything — low Re is honey-like laminar flow, high Re is chaotic turbulence. A swimming bacterium lives at Re ≈ 10⁻⁴ where water feels like tar; a whale at Re ≈ 10⁸.</li>
    <li><strong>Boundary layers:</strong> fluid sticks to surfaces (no-slip), and drag lives in the thin layer where speed ramps up. Golf ball dimples trip the layer to reduce drag — 30% further drives.</li>
    <li><strong>Turbulence is unsolved:</strong> the Navier–Stokes equations describe it perfectly, but proving they always behave smoothly is a $1-million Millennium Prize. Fluid dynamics is classical physics with a live frontier.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Bernoulli (1738); Feynman Lectures Vol. II, ch. 40–41; Batchelor, An Introduction to Fluid Dynamics.</p>
</TopicPage>
